# The Banned Game Name

the name of the block-building game every voxel engine gets compared to must not appear anywhere in this repo — not in code, type names, comments, docs prose, changelog copy, asset briefs, or commit messages. this is legal exposure, not style: this is a published package, and a match inside it gets the project banned.

there is no attribution exception here. TypeDoc bakes the engine README into the generated API pages, so a link that looks like it sits in an unpublished README ships anyway.

say "voxel game", "voxel engines", or "Voxelize" instead, and name the mechanic instead of the game it came from: "subchunk random-tick sampler", not "<that game>-style random ticks". a struct named after the game is the same violation as a comment, and a worse one, because renaming a public type later is a breaking change nobody wants to make under pressure.

the engine-boundary gate (`node scripts/engine-boundary/check.mjs`) carries the name as a digest and fails on it as a whole word, in any casing, in voxelize's own CI; a consumer's CI can also scan the submodule for it as a substring. check inherited code you are about to touch: any hit is a defect. fix it if it is in your reach, and say so out loud if it is not — a violation somebody else introduced is still a violation once you have seen it.
